WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:03.600
The doctor told you the truth last
clip, and one line stayed red:

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.067
the model key.

00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:07.533
Clear it now, and Claude starts
working inside your organization,

00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:09.867
holding a key that can
never approve anything.

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:13.033
Most teams hand an agent their
own admin login. You won't.

00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:16.533
By the end you'll have an organization,
a model key, an agent key,

00:00:16.533 --> 00:00:18.267
and Claude connected over MCP.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.467
Here's what that prevents. Leo gives
the agent his own admin session.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.767
Weeks later, the audit trail can't
tell Leo's changes from the agent's.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:28.167
And one customer's rows sit a single
bad query away from another's.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.167
Your data sits behind a
wall the database enforces,

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:35.367
and your agent holds a key that can
never approve. Four steps build it.

00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:38.900
Step one, the organization. A
key belongs to one organization,

00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:41.033
so the organization has to
exist before anything else.

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:44.233
In the console: open
localhost, port three thousand,

00:00:44.233 --> 00:00:46.300
and sign in as the owner.
The password is in the .

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.900
env file in your instance folder.
You land on Your organizations.

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:53.000
If you used gtm setup, your first
organization already exists.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.533
To make another, use the Create
organization card, type a name,

00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:56.100
and you're in.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.367
Anyone signed in can create one,
and you invite teammates by email,

00:00:59.367 --> 00:00:59.967
with a role.

00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:01.933
Or ask your agent: nothing yet.

00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:06.067
No key exists to carry the request,
so this one is a person's step.

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:09.567
Now the wall. Everything you store
is separated per organization,

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:11.233
and the database enforces
it, not the application.

00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:15.167
Every table that holds customer data
sits under Postgres row-level security,

00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:16.567
forced even for the table's owner.

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:20.000
The application connects as a database
role that cannot bypass those rules.

00:01:20.067 --> 00:01:24.100
So a query made for one organization
sees only that organization's rows.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.667
Your data stays yours.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.967
Step two clears your
red line: the model key.

00:01:27.967 --> 00:01:31.833
In the console: Integrations, then
Connections, then the Models category.

00:01:31.833 --> 00:01:33.733
Pick Anthropic and paste your key.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.600
The key is sealed with envelope
encryption under your master key,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.300
and it's live without a restart.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.633
A key you've saved but not tested
reads configured but unverified,

00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:42.567
so click Test.

00:01:42.567 --> 00:01:46.200
A key you add here also wins over any
instance wide key in the environment.

00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:49.000
Or ask your agent: "Which
model provider is live".

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.767
It reads the readiness answer,
and it never takes the key itself,

00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:53.533
because secrets never
pass through an agent.

00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:56.767
Run gtm doctor again, and
the model line reads live.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.200
Step three gives Claude
an identity of its own.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.333
In the console: Settings, then Developer.

00:02:01.333 --> 00:02:03.767
The page is titled Connect
Claude Code or Codex.

00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:06.767
Choose your coding agent,
name it, and set an expiry.

00:02:06.767 --> 00:02:08.033
Ninety days is the default.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.733
Then choose what it may do. Full GTM
engineer is the Claude Code preset.

00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:16.233
It configures nearly everything you
can. Approval, signing, key creation,

00:02:16.233 --> 00:02:18.633
OAuth sign ins and the Dry
Mode switch stay with people.

00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:21.900
The approve permission can't
be granted to a key at all.

00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:24.233
Click Create agent key.
The key is shown once.

00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:27.033
The command line does the
same with gtm token create.

00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:29.933
Pro move: the real limit is the
narrowest of three, the key's scopes,

00:02:29.933 --> 00:02:32.533
the agent's scopes, and
your own current role.

00:02:32.533 --> 00:02:34.700
Lose a role, and your
agents shrink with you.

00:02:34.733 --> 00:02:35.667
Or ask your agent:

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:38.800
"Create an agent key for yourself
and connect to this organization".

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.133
It mints the key in one call
with your owner session,

00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:44.033
so your session token and password
never land in the transcript.

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:45.733
Step four connects the folder.

00:02:45.733 --> 00:02:49.533
Put the key in an environment
variable named GTMOS_AGENT_TOKEN,

00:02:49.533 --> 00:02:53.800
never in a file. The . mcp. json holds
only a reference to that variable.

00:02:53.867 --> 00:02:57.267
Then run gtm init with the API
address and the key. Mind this one.

00:02:57.267 --> 00:03:00.167
Restart Claude Code
from that same terminal,

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:02.700
because it reads the
variable only at startup.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:05.533
Run /mcp, and the gtmos
server should show connected.

00:03:05.533 --> 00:03:07.133
If a key ever lands in a file,

00:03:07.133 --> 00:03:10.500
revoke it on the Developer
page or with gtm token revoke.

00:03:10.533 --> 00:03:14.567
Claude now has a small set of
tools: describe, query, get, search,

00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:16.133
act and approvals. Reads run directly.

00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:20.467
Anything that leaves the building
is proposed, and a person approves.

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.467
Check before you ask for anything else.

00:03:22.467 --> 00:03:25.433
Ask your agent: "Which organization
and key am I connected to".

00:03:25.433 --> 00:03:28.667
The answer names the agent, the
organization and the key's scopes.

00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:31.200
If it names the wrong
organization, stop and fix it.

00:03:31.233 --> 00:03:34.767
Every action is logged against the
agent, the key and the person behind it.

00:03:34.767 --> 00:03:37.000
In the console: Settings, then Audit log.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.967
Or ask your agent: "Show me the
audit entries for this key".

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:42.633
Now the first asks. Say:
"Start the GTM OS setup".

00:03:42.633 --> 00:03:46.033
Claude reads where setup stands
and resumes at the current step,

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:49.000
following the onboarding
skill one screen at a time.

00:03:49.033 --> 00:03:50.767
Then say: "Initialise
the onboarding agents".

00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:53.267
That means the onboarding
agent and the ops copilot,

00:03:53.267 --> 00:03:54.333
from the ops agents pack.

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:57.767
Enabling a pack is a proposal, and a
different owner or admin approves it.

00:03:57.767 --> 00:04:00.067
In the console: Settings,
Packages, then Approvals.

00:04:00.067 --> 00:04:02.033
Claude gives you the approval number.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:05.967
A careful engineer will say: an agent
with a key into my customer data?

00:04:05.967 --> 00:04:08.567
The key reads and proposes,
and it can't approve.

00:04:08.567 --> 00:04:10.467
Three limits cap it, it expires,

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:13.733
and the database keeps every
query inside one organization.

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:16.867
Checkpoint: Create the
organization, add the model key,

00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:20.800
mint a narrow agent key, connect over
MCP. Your data sits behind a wall,

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.433
and your agent holds a key
that can never approve.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:26.700
Your agent now has a private
organization and its first tasks.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.000
Next: the onboarding steps,

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.800
six screens that turn your
answers into definitions.
